WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.189 --> 00:00:08.370
Time is an illusion, lunchtime doubly so.
So what time do you make it right now? Probably

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.990
every single person watching will have a different
answer, not just because you watched this

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:16.510
video at different times during the day, but
also because we have to deal with time zones,

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:21.510
different clocks and varying degrees of accuracy,
but mostly because you bought that suspicious

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:27.580
“Rilex” watch for $5 in Singapore.
The idea of time existed long before clocks

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:33.500
so how do we define it, what is time and what
are some of the amazing and strange ways it

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:41.829
can affect our lives?
Early attitudes towards time were relatively

00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:46.039
simple; we used the motions of the sun to
give us an understanding of our place in the

00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:53.469
day. Sun high; find food, kill food, eat food.
Sun low, find woman, go to cave, shout at

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:58.019
woman till she make fire. There were also
the shifting seasons which we weren’t able

00:00:58.019 --> 00:01:02.199
to measure but you would learn to follow them
and predict them from the way that nature

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:06.460
changed around them.
The first instruments to make time measurements

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:12.410
were sundials. But these were highly localised
and, from our perspective as clock users,

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:17.110
the times would be different each day since
the amount of daylight increases and decreases

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:22.100
steadily throughout the year, by just over
2 minutes per day in fact. This means that

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.960
a specific point on the sundial would actually
be reached slightly early or later the following

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.840
day.
There were various other measures, like sand

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.790
timers and water clocks, or just counting
how many teeth you had left. It wasn’t until

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:39.971
the early 19th century that we had reliable
mechanical clocks. Still, they weren’t all

00:01:39.971 --> 00:01:44.910
running at the same speed, but at this point,
it didn’t really matter. People in each

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:50.450
town worked to one main clock, and the bells
would ring out every hour on the hour and

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:54.720
that’s how the day would be organised. Then,
when you went to a different town, you would

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.050
then work off that clock, which might run
at a different speed. But the difference was

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:04.880
so small that you wouldn’t notice the change.
Then, came along one significant technology

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.020
that made us realise that we had to synch
up all of these different clocks. And that

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:15.160
thing was railways. Imagine trying to organise
a train schedule where every stop on the line

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:21.030
thinks it’s a different time, partly because
of their own town clock but also due to their

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:26.950
different positions on the globe. A city like
Bristol, only a couple of hour’s car journey

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:31.660
West of London, would have a solar noon, that’s
the point when the sun is at its highest in

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:39.040
the sky, about 10 minutes later than London
thanks to the 2.5 degree difference in position.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:47.130
So we needed a standard or it would be chaos.
On December 1st 1847, time really began, with

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:52.910
the adoption of Greenwich Mean Time, which
was often referred to as railway time. Until

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:59.080
1880, when it became all of Britain’s legal
time, many clocks would show both local and

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:04.380
GMT on separate faces. The time zones in the
US became vital since the differences over

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:09.550
such a large country were even greater. That’s
also where the word “hello” comes from.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:13.950
A telephone greeting needed to be adopted,
rather than good morning or good afternoon,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:18.500
since you could be talking to someone thousands
of miles away and their morning might be your

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:24.700
afternoon. Alexander Graham Bell, inventor
of the telephone, preferred the phrase “ahoy-hoy”.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:28.050
Imagine how much more fun the world would
have been if that had stuck.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:33.400
Soon each county in Britain had standardised
time and then the world began to coordinate

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:40.740
as communication and travel became ever faster.
Most countries related their time to GMT by

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:47.930
1929 but it wasn’t until Nepal signed up
in 1986 that we had a complete global system.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:54.410
Okay, so that’s the basic history, now let’s
talk about the theory. What exactly is time?

00:03:54.410 --> 00:04:00.300
Or at least, how can we define it? Well time
is really three things or concepts that share

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:05.280
a common name.
First, it is a way to label and order events

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.819
in the universe. I buy a tub of ice cream,
I eat all the ice cream, I feel sick and slightly

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:16.090
ashamed of myself. I need something that can
show that buying comes before eating, and

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:21.400
then sickness and shame follow it. This is
ordering, like a calendar.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:25.600
Second, it’s a way to measure the distance
between two events. The ordering tells me

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.080
which comes first but it doesn’t say how
far apart they are. If I get a new job and

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.350
then a new car will they happen 2 weeks or
2 months apart?

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:40.090
And thirdly, and this one is a little more
abstract, time is a medium we move through

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:45.340
that allows us to see that change has actually
happened. The leaves turn from green to orange,

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:49.530
wrinkles grow under your eyes and that beer
belly swells ever larger. This gives us the

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:54.800
concept of past and future. It’s interesting
that the Ancient Greeks saw the future as

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.680
something that snuck up, unseen, behind their
backs, while the past was laid in front of

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:04.900
them, for them to stare out into. Sneaky future,
you never know what it’s up to.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:09.510
This third concept is really important because
it’s the only one that requires us to have

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:15.729
an arrow of time, a direction it moves in
and never changes. The future cannot come

00:05:15.729 --> 00:05:20.580
before the present; the smoke never returns
from the air into the cigarette, the milk

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:26.020
never un-swirls out of our coffee and I don’t
eject that ice cream into the tub, walk backwards

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:30.240
to the shop and exchange it for money, that
would be weird and gross.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:36.680
The reason many things seem strange when you
play them backwards is because of Entropy.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:42.389
Entropy is the second law of thermodynamics
and of all the rules of physics, it’s probably

00:05:42.389 --> 00:05:48.760
the one we are surest about. To put it simply,
entropy is the measure of the order or disorder

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:54.600
of a system. The law states that the entropy
of a closed system will either remain constant

00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:01.081
or increase with time. This means that you
always go from ordered to disordered. Think

00:06:01.081 --> 00:06:06.419
about your bedroom. You tidy it and then it
always slips slowly into a chaotic mess. And

00:06:06.419 --> 00:06:10.870
say in that mess you find a chocolate bar;
that is a nice, ordered, delicious collection

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:15.810
of energy. But then you stuff it in your face
and that stored calorie energy turns into

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:20.580
kinetic energy for walking, heat, even sound
for when you tread on something in the dark

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:25.680
and scream. I told you your room was messy.
At least now you know it’s not your fault.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.610
It’s just entropy.
Think about a coffee with milk in it. The

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:33.180
milk will always stay mixed throughout the
coffee, it will never separate so you have

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:37.190
an area in the cup of just milk and an area
of just coffee. If it does, then your milk

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:40.740
was really out of date and has evolved into
a sentient being.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:46.350
But why is this? Well, it’s really just
probability. The amount of ways you can organise

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:51.980
something into a useful or ordered way, is
much much smaller than the ways you can organise

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:57.020
it into a chaotic, meaningless way.
Let’s take writing as an example. If you

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:01.650
close your eyes and randomly bash letters
on your keyboard for a minute, it’s unlikely

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:06.750
that you will make many words because there
are so many combinations of letters but only

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:13.960
a few combinations make real words. Take the
word “Piggy”, “P I G G Y” there are

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:20.290
no other words in English made from just those
5 letters; yiggp, giypg, pygig, no.

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:26.669
One more example. Put an ice cube in a bowl
of water and it will melt and you’ll end

00:07:26.669 --> 00:07:31.699
up with a bowl full of slightly more water.
Now if you reversed time, you would expect

00:07:31.699 --> 00:07:36.650
the ice cube to form out of the water; but
how would this be possible? How would all

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:41.720
those water molecules decide which of them
had to become the ice and which would stay

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:47.050
as water? It doesn’t make sense. Entropy
is how we know that time is destined to move

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:51.530
in one direction.
Alright so we know that time has a direction

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:56.319
but what about a speed? Is time moving at
the same rate for everyone and everything?

00:07:56.319 --> 00:08:02.460
No, it isn’t, it’s relative to the speed
you are moving and by gravity. The faster

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:07.580
you are moving, the slower you experience
time compared to someone who is not moving.

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:14.040
This is part of Einstein’s Theory of Relativity.
You can see relativity in action every day.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:18.050
For example, if you’re on a moving train
and you throw a ball up in the air then catch

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:22.280
it, you only witness the ball moving at the
speed you threw it. Whereas someone stood

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:26.970
on the platform as the train shoots past them
at high speed, watching you throw the ball

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:31.690
in the air through the window, will see the
ball move much faster. Because they will see

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:36.700
it move at the speed you threw the ball plus
the speed of the moving train.

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:42.050
Now, the problem comes when we swap the ball
for a beam of light. Most of the time the

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:46.730
speed of light is fixed, although it can be
slowed down nothing can travel faster than

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:53.060
it. So take the same example but swap the
ball for a beam of light, the train is moving

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:57.120
and you throw the beam of light up in the
air. So in this instance the onlooker would

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:03.130
see the light beam travel at the speed of
light plus the speed of the train. But that’s

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:08.360
not possible because you can’t exceed the
speed of light. So something must have happened

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:14.839
to the time to make up for the lost speed,
therefore in this example, you and the onlooker

00:09:14.839 --> 00:09:19.100
would be experiencing time passing at a different
rate.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:23.370
This is called time dilation and it’s a
great excuse for being late, since you can

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:29.620
claim your watch was just going slower because
you walk so fast. But in reality, this difference

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:34.950
is negligible. The astronauts racing around
the earth at almost 8km/s in the ISS have

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:42.490
aged about 0.005 seconds less than us back
on Earth in the past 6 months. Gravity also

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:48.360
affects the flow of time in the same fashion.
The stronger the gravity the slower time passes.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:53.170
So if you were stood on a planet with several
hundred times the mass of Earth for just one

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:58.180
hour, a whole year will have passed back here
on Earth.

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:02.620
Relativity will never truly have an impact
on our lives, until we can build ships that

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:07.550
can move at close to light speed. Then, you
could go off on a trip to some star and come

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:13.060
back to find all of your relatives long dead
and buried and a bill of £14 billion because

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:15.300
you forgot to cancel your phone contract.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:20.640
That’s the physics side of things but what
about personal relativity. Why is it that

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:26.640
your current situation affects how fast that
you feel time is passing you by. One hour

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:31.390
in a chemistry class definitely doesn’t
feel the same as one hour out in a bar, unless

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:34.700
you really love chemistry or you’re rubbish
at drinking.

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:38.540
When you’re a kid, summer seems to stretch
on forever but as you grow into adulthood,

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:42.810
summer is gone in a blink and you can’t
remember what year it is and you realise that

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:47.830
you missed your mum’s birthday again. And
what about those rare moments of extreme danger

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:52.660
or intensity? If you’ve ever been in a serious
accident, you’ll remember that feeling of

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:58.180
time slowing almost to a halt and it seems
like you’re processing everything at a far

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:03.220
quicker rate than ever before.
A neuroscientist called David Eagleman became

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:08.660
fascinated with this idea and with how the
brain measures time. Your brain is incredibly

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:13.680
accurate when it comes to time. Take sound
for example. When you hear a loud bang, you

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:18.330
turn your head towards the source of the sound,
but how do you know where it’s coming from?

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:23.170
Well, that sound travels from the source and
hits each of your ears at a slightly different

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:27.980
time, thanks to the width of your head, which
is how far apart your ears are. This time

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:33.899
difference is as little as 9 microseconds,
that’s 9 millionths of a second, but your

00:11:33.899 --> 00:11:39.450
subconscious brain is still able to recognise
and use this information to calculate where

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:44.260
the sound is coming from. You also track the
rhythms of the day and night, without needing

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:52.779
to see the sun. Your brain can measure seconds,
minutes and hours passing by with incredible

00:11:52.779 --> 00:11:59.290
accuracy. Ever woken up exactly 1 minute before
your alarm goes off? That’s no coincidence,

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:03.790
your brain is like a little Swiss watchmaker’s
house, stuffed full of various time keeping

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:08.550
devices, and possibly some cheese.
Eagleman tried to test the effect of high

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:13.040
intensity situations. The problem was, you
can’t just go around pushing people in front

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:18.220
of cars or dropping their pets out of windows
to see how they react. So he found something

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:22.779
called a suspended catch air device, that
basically involves dropping people through

00:12:22.779 --> 00:12:28.130
a hole, while they face upwards so they can’t
see the safety net behind them. He gave each

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:33.130
volunteer a screen on their wrist, which would
flash a random number slightly faster than

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:38.400
the eye can normally see. If they did experience
time slower, then hopefully they’d be able

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:44.170
to see the number. Sadly, it didn’t work
but they did note that when asked how long

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:51.779
they thought the fall had taken, volunteers
overestimated by an average of 36% so something

00:12:51.779 --> 00:12:57.329
was happening to their perception of time,
but this phenomenon still largely remains

00:12:57.329 --> 00:13:01.050
a mystery.
And finally, we can’t talk about time without

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:07.010
mentioning time travel. Travelling to the
future is clearly possible, thanks to special

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:11.151
relativity, all you need to do is keep travelling
close to the speed of light and life on earth

00:13:11.151 --> 00:13:16.870
will race by much quicker than your personal
experience of time. Traveling close to light

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:23.269
speed is no easy task though, it would require
absolutely enormous amounts of energy. And

00:13:23.269 --> 00:13:27.589
imagine how disappointed you’d be if you
got out your ship and still no one had invented

00:13:27.589 --> 00:13:32.830
x-ray glasses or a reverse microwave to make
your beer cold in an instant. Without those,

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:38.060
the future is totally pointless.
Travelling to the past is a little trickier

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:42.649
and we’d almost certainly need some sort
of wormhole to achieve it. But it’s at least

00:13:42.649 --> 00:13:48.470
theoretically possible, having a wormhole
to the past would not defy the laws of physics,

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:54.210
even if it does lead to the potential for
paradoxes, such as the bootstrap paradox.

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:58.500
This could be where a young man travels back
in time, meets a girl and gets her pregnant.

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:02.920
He leaves, because he didn’t get into the
time travel game to get tied down. But the

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:09.519
woman gives birth to a boy, who ends up being
the time traveller. He is, without knowing

00:14:09.519 --> 00:14:15.029
it, his own dad. It would make parents evening
at school very confusing.

00:14:15.029 --> 00:14:21.480
So time is many things, it can be a measurement,
a concept and even a way of travel. We still

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:26.300
don’t fully understand the complexities
of time and maybe we never will. But in the

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:41.430
end it doesn’t matter what it is, or how
long it is, it’s what you do with 

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:53.840
it that counts.

